In a directory with checkers.json, the compiler commands first fetch the package's
dependencies and update checkers.lock; the file defaults to main.ck.
`ckc` is found through CKC, else next to `checkers`, else on PATH.
-- exit 2
$ ckc trust examples/wc.ck
-- stdout
-- stderr
error: unknown command `trust`

ckc — the Checkers compiler (usually run through `checkers`, the build tool)

USAGE:
    ckc run [--threads N] [--overlay MAP] <FILE.ck> [ARG]...
                                               check and prove, then run `main` with ARGs
                                               (N worker threads, default: cores; 1 is deterministic)
    ckc build [--release] <FILE.ck> -o <PROGRAM>
                                               check and prove, then write an executable
                                               (its threads: CHECKERS_THREADS, default: cores);
                                               --release refuses any `admit` the program relies on
    ckc check [--deny-warnings] <FILE.ck>...   type-check and run `check`s; report diagnostics
    ckc check --json [--overlay MAP] <FILE.ck> the same as JSON lines, phase by phase, for tools (§15.5);
                                               MAP is a JSON object from paths to unsaved text
                                               (`-`: read it from standard input)

    check, run, build and stage reuse Z3's answers from `.checkers/` beside the
    file; --no-cache (before the file) or CHECKERS_NO_CACHE=1 turns that off, and
    CHECKERS_CACHE_STATS=1 prints how many were reused, on standard error.

    Before the file, packages (`checkers` passes these from checkers.json):
      --package NAME=DIR    a dependency, importable as `NAME.module`
      --uses PKG=A,B        the packages PKG may import (`.` is this program's own)
    ckc complete [--overlay MAP] <FILE.ck> LINE COL
                                               what can be written at LINE, COL (from 0, the
                                               column in bytes), as JSON lines (§15.5)
    ckc index [--overlay MAP] <FILE.ck>        the names of every module of FILE's package
                                               (the files under the directory its module
                                               name is relative to), as JSON lines
    ckc stage <FILE.ck>                        print the code staging generates (§8.9)
    ckc parse <FILE.ck>                        print the syntax tree
    ckc --version

-- exit 2
$ checkers build --release tests/tool/admitted.ck -o tests/tool/admitted.out-never-written
